For $m=0$, $r_{0}=0$ and $H_0^{r_{0}}=H_{0}^{-}$, so
$X_{0,n}=r_{n}$.

For $b\in H_{m}$ consider the space--time translation $\theta_{b,m}:(x,i)\longmapsto(x+b,\,i+m)$. Since $b\in H_{m}$, $(x+b)+(i+m)$ is even exactly
when $x+i$ is. Therefore the map $\theta_{b,m}$ preserves $\widetilde{\mathbb{Z}}^{2}_{+}$, and it sends
$H_{0}^{-}$ onto $H_{m}^{b}$. The environment and the uniform variables at heights $>m$ are independent of
$\mathcal{F}_{m}$, and their joint law is invariant under $\theta_{b,m}$, since $\xi$ is
stationary in the time variable and the variables $(U_{z})$ are i.i.d. Moreover, since the first site of an open path is not required to be
open, $\zeta_{m,n}$ is a function of the configuration above height $m$. Applying this with the $\mathcal{F}_{m}$-measurable shift $b=r_{m}\in H_{m}$, we obtain that, conditionally on $\mathcal{F}_{m}$,
\[
  (X_{m,m+j})_{j\ge1}\ \overset{\text{law}}{=}\ (r_{j})_{j\ge1}.
\]

For the almost sure convergence, we apply Liggett's subadditive ergodic theorem
\cite[Theorem~1.10]{Liggett1985} to the array $(X_{m,n})$. Hypothesis (1.7) is
exactly~\eqref{eq:X}, since $X_{0,m}=r_{m}$. Hypothesis (1.8) is the above
identification of the conditional law of $(X_{m,m+j})_{j\ge1}$, which does not
depend on $m$. As that conditional law does not depend on the conditioning
either, $X_{m,n}$ is independent of $\mathcal{F}_{m}$. The variables
$(X_{jk,(j+1)k})_{j\ge1}$ are therefore i.i.d., which is (1.9). As Liggett
remarks~\cite[p.~1280]{Liggett1985}, (1.3) can be weakened to
$\mathbb{E}[X_{0,1}^{+}]<\infty$, at the price of allowing the limit in (1.4) to be
$-\infty$ and of dropping the $L^{1}$ convergence in (1.5). This weaker
assumption holds here since $X_{0,1}=r_{1}\le1$. The processes in (1.9) being
i.i.d., hence ergodic, (1.4) and (1.5) give that $r_{n}/n$ converges almost
surely to the constant
\[
  \alpha(\delta):=\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{a_{n}}{n}=\inf_{n\ge1}\frac{a_{n}}{n}
  \ \in\ [-\infty,1].
\]

