For every $k$, applying Lemma~\ref{lem:basic} to $\{x\in H_{0}:x\le-2k\}$ shows
that $B_{m}$ almost surely contains a point at $x$-coordinate at most $-2k+m$,
since the $x$-coordinate increases by at most $1$ per step. Intersecting over
$k$, the sets $B_{m}\subseteq G_{m}$ are almost surely infinite and not bounded
from below, while $\max G_{m}\le r_{m-1}+1<\infty$. Since the first site of an
open path is not required to be open, $\zeta_{m,n}$ depends only on heights
$m+1,\dots,n$, where the two occupation configurations are the same. Therefore, it is the
same map in both configurations and, by~\eqref{eq:semigroup}, $\Delta_{m}r_{n}=\max\zeta_{m,n}(G_{m})-\max\zeta_{m,n}(B_{m})$.

Conditionally on $\widehat{\mathcal{F}}_{m}$, the sets $B_{m}\subseteq G_{m}$
are fixed, the environment above height $m$ is fixed, and $\zeta_{m,n}$ is
generated by the variables $U_{z}$ at heights $>m$, so that its conditional law
is $\mathbb{P}^{\xi}$. Applying Lemma~\ref{lem:gain} conditionally on
$\widehat{\mathcal{F}}_{m}$, and using $\Delta_{m}r_{n}\ge0$ on the
complementary event,
\[
  \mathbb{E}\bigl[\Delta_{m}r_{n}\mid\widehat{\mathcal{F}}_{m}\bigr]
  \ \ge\ 2\cdot\mathbf{1}\{\max G_{m}>\max B_{m}\}.
\]
Taking expectations and using~\eqref{eq:pivot} gives the claim.

