Write $j=Ml+t$ with $0\le t<M$ and $l\le n/M$. By~\eqref{eq:X},
$r_{j}\le r_{Ml}+X_{Ml,j}$, and $X_{Ml,j}\le j-Ml=t$ since the $x$-coordinate
increases by at most $1$ at each level. Here, our model differs
from~\cite[Lemma~18]{ArcherEtAl2026}, where the increments of the right edge are
only dominated by geometric variables. Hence, if $r_{j}>\alpha j+\rho n$, then
\[
  r_{Ml}>\alpha j+\rho n-t\ \ge\ \alpha Ml+\rho n-M\ \ge\ \alpha Ml+\frac{\rho}{2}n
\]
as soon as $n\ge2M/\rho$.

Iterating~\eqref{eq:X} along the intervals $[M(i-1),Mi]$ and using $r_{0}=0$
gives
\[
  r_{Ml}\ \le\ \sum_{i=1}^{l}X_{M(i-1),Mi},
\]
where, by the proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:speeds}, the variables
$X_{M(i-1),Mi}$, $i\ge1$, are i.i.d.\ with the law of $r_{M}$. Define
\[
  Z_{i}:=X_{M(i-1),Mi}-\Bigl(\alpha+\frac{\rho}{4}\Bigr)M ,
  \qquad
  S_{l}:=\sum_{i=1}^{l}Z_{i}\ \ge\ r_{Ml}-\Bigl(\alpha+\frac{\rho}{4}\Bigr)Ml.
\]
On the event $\{r_{j}>\alpha j+\rho n\}$ we thus have
$S_{l}>\rho n/2-\rho Ml/4\ge\rho n/4$, since $Ml\le n$.
If $l=0$, then $S_{0}=0$ and this event is empty.

Since $r_{M}\le M$, the random variables $Z_{i}$ are bounded from above, and they
are integrable, by Lemma~\ref{lem:basic}, with $\mathbb{E}[Z_{1}]<0$
by~\eqref{eq:choiceM}. So there exists $\lambda>0$ such that
$\mathbb{E}[e^{\lambda Z_{1}}]<1$. For $l\ge1$, Chernoff's bound then gives
\[
  \mathbb{P}\Bigl(S_{l}>\frac{\rho}{4}n\Bigr)
  \le e^{-\lambda\rho n/4}\,\mathbb{E}\bigl[e^{\lambda Z_{1}}\bigr]^{l}
  <e^{-\lambda\rho n/4}.
\]
This proves~\eqref{eq:tailk} with $c:=\lambda\rho/4$ and $n_{0}\ge2M/\rho$.

